.TH OBSERVER 1 "2026-08-27" "observer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
observer \- manage this machine's Observer service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B observer
.RI [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B observer
manages the machine token of the Observer service running on this computer.
.PP
To watch the machine you are sitting at you need no token at all: the dashboard
comes in through the local socket, and membership of the
.B observer
group is what grants access to it. The token exists only so that
.I another
computer can query this one.
.PP
The service generates its own token the first time it starts and keeps it in the
credential store, so nothing has to be configured after installation. This
command never takes a token as an argument, which is why no token ever reaches
your shell history.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B share " [\-\-stdout]"
Print the machine token, with the context needed to install it on another
computer. Requires privileges, because the credential store is readable only by
the service account and by root.
.IP
With
.B \-\-stdout
it prints the token alone, with no trailing newline, for capture in a shell
variable. The newline matters: a trailing one would enter the value and be
rejected byte by byte by the constant-time comparison.
.TP
.B rotate\-key
Replace the machine token. The previous one keeps working for 24 hours, so
remote clients are not cut off all at once. Requires privileges.
.TP
.B doctor
Report where the credential store is, how well it is protected, what identity
this terminal has, and what a client would see on the local channel. Requires
nothing, and it is the command that explains the other two when they fail.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The credential store could not be read or written.
.TP
.B 2
The command was not recognised.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/observer/credentials.json
The credential store. Mode 0600, owned by the
.B observer
system user.
.TP
.I /run/observer/observer.sock
The local channel. Callers that are local and identifiable are served without
any token.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR observer-dashboard (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
